<header class="page-header">
  <div class="section-label">The Flagship Expression</div>
  <h1>NewComm</h1>
  <p class="page-desc">The New Community Project is Literary Science&rsquo;s flagship expression&mdash;and the proof of what happens when the methodology transforms what an institution&rsquo;s campus is for.</p>
</header>

<div class="narrative reveal">
  <p><strong>NewComm operates on the campuses of independent schools&mdash;Horace Mann, Poly Prep&mdash;institutions with extraordinary assets:</strong> physical infrastructure built for deep learning, faculty who became educators because they believe in young people, networks that span industries, and the kind of institutional credibility that opens doors. NewComm&rsquo;s premise is that all of that can serve a larger purpose than it currently does.</p>

  <p>These campuses become shared sites of knowledge-making&mdash;spaces where students from historically marginalized communities and the host institution&rsquo;s own community work alongside each other on challenges that matter to both. Literary Science is the methodology that makes this more than a partnership on paper. It provides the intellectual architecture&mdash;novels as operational technology, community data as evidence, hypothesis as the bridge to action&mdash;so that what happens on campus produces real outcomes for the communities beyond the gate.</p>

  <p>Every year, a client organization presents a real community challenge. Fellows&mdash;high school students from historically marginalized communities&mdash;spend a five-week Summer Intensive developing hypotheses through Literary Science: reading novels as operational technology, overlaying them onto community data, and synthesizing both into specific, testable claims about what could change. When the school year begins, student-led Project Teams enter the monthly Loop&mdash;a twelve-month cycle of experimentation and execution that culminates in $10,000 community interventions designed, prototyped, and delivered by the students themselves.</p>

  <p>No one hands them a lesson plan. The project is the teacher. The community is the classroom. And the campus&mdash;with all of its resources, its people, and its potential&mdash;becomes the infrastructure that makes the work possible. The result is that everyone in the building benefits: the host institution discovers what its space can do when the gate opens, and the community discovers what becomes possible when institutional assets are shared.</p>
</div>

